    "### Your explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "**What does one row represent, and what are the two targets?**\n",
    "\n",
    "One row is one hotel booking (a reservation) at either the Resort Hotel or the City Hotel in Portugal. The file has 119,390 bookings and 32 columns, covering arrivals from July 2015 to August 2017. We have two targets. The first is `adr`, the average daily rate (roughly how much was paid per night), which is a number, so that's the regression task. The second is `is_canceled`, where 1 means the booking got canceled and 0 means it didn't. That's the classification task. About 37% of all bookings in the file were canceled, so the classes aren't perfectly balanced but they're not extreme either.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What do the source documentation and imported types tell you about the features?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Most columns came in as normal numbers or text, but a few needed attention. `children` was read as text instead of a number because 4 rows contain the literal text `NA`. I loaded the file with pandas' default missing-value handling turned off on purpose, because the dictionary says `NULL` in `agent` and `company` means \"not applicable\" (no agent or no company was involved), which is different from \"unknown\". If pandas had auto-converted every `NULL` to a missing value, those two meanings would have been mixed up. The documentation also tells us that `agent` and `company` are ID codes (labels, not quantities), that `reservation_status` and its date reveal the final outcome so they can't be inputs, and that the data was put together roughly one day before arrival, so we should call this retrospective prediction."
   ]
  },
