    "### Your explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "**How do the three partitions serve different purposes?**\n",
    "\n",
    "The training set (2,999 rows, about 60%) is the only data used to fit anything: models, scalers, encoders, imputation values and outlier thresholds. The validation set (1,001 rows, about 20%) is what we use to compare settings and pick the best model, so it's allowed to influence our choices. The test set (1,000 rows, about 20%) is kept locked away until the very end, so that the final score is an honest estimate of how the model does on bookings it has never seen. If we tuned on the test set it would stop being a fair test.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What checks demonstrate that your split avoids overlap and preserves useful class representation?**\n",
    "\n",
    "First, I took a reproducible random sample of 5,000 rows (seed 432) from the 118,564 rows that passed the fixed validity rules. Then I grouped rows that have identical predictor values (without looking at either target) and used a stratified, group-aware split, so identical bookings always end up in the same partition. In the sample, 840 rows share their predictor values with another row, so this really matters. The checks printed above show 0 shared rows and 0 shared predictor groups between every pair of partitions. After cleaning I checked again with the cleaned values and still found 0 identical records across partitions. For class balance, the canceled share is 37.3% in train, 37.4% in validation and 37.4% in test, against 37.4% for the whole sample, so each partition has a very similar mix. The row indexes are saved in `split_membership.csv`, so every model reuses exactly the same rows."
   ]
