   "source": [
    "### Your explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "**What evidence supports your treatment or decision to retain the values?**\n",
    "\n",
    "The counts above show only four columns with unknown values. `children` has 4 `NA` rows in the whole file, `market_segment` has 2 `Undefined` rows, `distribution_channel` has 5 and `country` has 488 `NULL` rows. The targets have no missing labels at all, so nothing needed to be imputed there (and we would never impute a target anyway). When the 5,000-row sample was drawn, none of the `children`, `market_segment` or `distribution_channel` problem rows ended up in it, so for those columns the fill rule changed nothing in this sample. I still built it, learned only from the training rows (children = 0, market segment = Online TA, distribution channel = TA/TO), and applied the same values to all three partitions, so the pipeline works if those rows show up again. For `country`, 24 sample rows (11 train, 4 validation, 9 test) were unknown. I did not fill them with the most common country because that would pretend we know where those guests are from (a wrong guess could mislead the model). I gave them their own label, `Unknown`. After the treatment there are 0 NaN cells left in any partition."
