   "source": [
    "### Your explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "**What evidence supports your treatment or decision to retain the values?**\n",
    "\n",
    "The `NULL` in `agent` and `company` is not really missing data. In the training rows, `agent` is NULL for 91% of Corporate bookings and 89% of Aviation, but for almost 0% of Online TA bookings, which makes sense because online travel agency bookings always have an agent and corporate or direct ones usually don't. `company` is NULL for 93.7% of all training rows, which fits the idea that most guests aren't booking through a company. This matches the dictionary, so I relabeled both as `Not applicable` and kept them as a category instead of filling them in. I also checked for hidden zero codes: there are no `0` values in `agent` or `company`. `meal = Undefined` (58 sample rows) means the same as `SC` (no meal package) according to the dictionary, so I merged them.\n",
    "\n",
    "For other zero values, I decided to keep them. In training, 26 rows (0.9%) have `adr` = 0 and 13 of those are Complementary bookings (13 out of 19 Complementary rows), so these look like genuinely free stays. The other zeros come from Groups, Offline TA/TO and Direct bookings, and I can't tell if those are discounts, vouchers or data errors, so I keep them and mention it as a limitation. 10 sample rows have 0 adults but all of them list children or babies, 268 bookings were made on the arrival day (lead time 0, which is a real value), and the `is_repeated_guest` flag disagrees with the booking history in 274 sample rows (267 are flagged as not repeated but have previous bookings). That is a quirk of the source and I can't tell which field is correct, so I left both alone."
   ]
  },
