   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cell-028",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Your explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "**What evidence supports your treatment or decision to retain the values?**\n",
    "\n",
    "I looked at the training rows only to learn thresholds. The extreme values are rare but not impossible, so I didn't delete them (deleting rows would also break the shared split). Instead I capped the input columns at the training 99.9th percentile: lead time at 581 days, weekend nights at 6, week nights at 15, adults at 4, children at 2, babies at 1, previous cancellations at 14 and previous non-canceled bookings at 22. Overall 28 values were clipped (13 in train, 5 in validation, 10 in test), and the same caps were applied to all partitions. The target `adr` was not changed. The highest `adr` in training is 383, and 11 training rows are above the 3\u00d7IQR fence (288), and the five highest are all Resort Hotel bookings in July or August, which is peak season, so high prices make sense. Changing the target would hide real error, and we select models with MAE, which isn't as sensitive to a few large values as RMSE. The really extreme rows in the full file (the 5,400 `adr`, the groups of 20 to 55 adults) did not end up in our sample, so they didn't need any treatment here, but the rule would cap the adult counts if they did."
   ]
  },
