    "### Your explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "**What cleaning and preparation did you apply, and which uncertainties remain?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Before splitting, I removed 826 rows that can't be real stays (1 negative `adr`, 715 zero-night stays, 110 more with zero guests), leaving 118,564 eligible rows, and then I took 5,000 of them at random. After the split I handled the unknown values, merged `meal` Undefined into SC, labeled `agent`/`company` NULL as Not applicable, fixed the data types, built and checked the arrival dates, and capped extreme input values using training-only thresholds. The inputs are then scaled and one-hot encoded with a pipeline fitted on the training rows only (60 model inputs from 20 predictor columns). No rows were dropped after the split, so the three partitions keep their sizes (2,999 / 1,001 / 1,000), and `raw_data` is unchanged (checked with a fingerprint). Some uncertainties remain: the zero-`adr` rows that aren't Complementary, the repeat-guest flag not always matching the history, the week numbers not following ISO, `country` having 24 unknown sample rows, and the fact that the sample has none of the most extreme rows from the full file.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Which training-data patterns support your modeling choices?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Cancellation goes up steadily with lead time: 10% for bookings made within a week, 27% for 8 to 30 days, 47% for 91 to 180 days and 61% for more than a year ahead, so lead time should help the classifiers. Cancellation also depends on the market segment (Groups 59%, Online TA 37%, Direct 15%, Complementary 5%), so segment is a useful category. ADR is right-skewed with a median of about 95 and it depends strongly on season and hotel: the Resort Hotel's mean ADR goes from about 51 in January to about 192 in August while the City Hotel stays between about 84 and 119, so the month and hotel are important for regression. Lead time itself has only a weak, slightly negative link with ADR, so a straight line on lead time won't explain price very well. This supports using scaled inputs for the distance-based and regularized models, and the one-hot month and hotel columns for all of them. We only looked at the training rows here and never at the test outcomes."
   ]
  },
